window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.5","title":"Normal Approximation to the Binomial","goal":"Use the airline that books 200 people (10% no-shows) to see how well a normal curve approximates the exact binomial answer for fewer than 15 no-shows.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Check the condition","text":"The normal curve is a good stand-in for the binomial only when both np and n(1 - p) are at least 10. Here n = 200 and p = 0.1, so we check both numbers first.","code":"n, p = 200, 0.1\n\nprint('np      =', n * p)\nprint('n(1-p)  =', n * (1 - p))\nprint('safe to approximate?', n * p >= 10 and n * (1 - p) >= 10)","out":"np      = 20.0\nn(1-p)  = 180.0\nsafe to approximate? True","figs":[]},{"title":"The exact binomial answer","text":"Fewer than 15 no-shows means 14 or fewer. The binomial cdf adds up all fifteen terms for us, so this is the true value we want the normal curve to match.","code":"from scipy import stats\n\nexact = stats.binom.cdf(14, n, p)\n\nprint(f'exact P(X <= 14) = {exact:.4f}')","out":"exact P(X <= 14) = 0.0929","figs":[]},{"title":"The normal approximation","text":"Match the normal curve to the binomial's mean np and SD sqrt(np(1 - p)). The continuity correction uses 14.5 because the bar for 14 stretches from 13.5 to 14.5.","code":"from math import sqrt\n\nmu = n * p\nsigma = sqrt(n * p * (1 - p))\n\nwith_cc = stats.norm.cdf(14.5, mu, sigma)\nwithout = stats.norm.cdf(14, mu, sigma)\n\nprint(f'mu = {mu:.1f}, sigma = {sigma:.3f}')\nprint(f'normal with correction    = {with_cc:.4f}')\nprint(f'normal without correction = {without:.4f}')","out":"mu = 20.0, sigma = 4.243\nnormal with correction    = 0.0974\nnormal without correction = 0.0786","figs":[]},{"title":"How close is it?","text":"The error with the half-unit correction is far smaller than without it. That is why the correction matters, even for a big n.","code":"print(f'error with correction    = {abs(with_cc - exact):.4f}')\nprint(f'error without correction = {abs(without - exact):.4f}')","out":"error with correction    = 0.0045\nerror without correction = 0.0143","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"n <- 200; p <- 0.1\npbinom(14, n, p)\npnorm(14.5, mean = n * p, sd = sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)))\npnorm(14,   mean = n * p, sd = sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)))","expect":"pbinom: 0.0929 (exact); pnorm with 14.5: 0.0974; pnorm with 14: 0.0786","note":"pbinom is the exact answer. The two pnorm lines are the approximation with and without the continuity correction."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use CDF.BINOM and CDF.NORMAL)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE exact = CDF.BINOM(14, 200, 0.1).\nCOMPUTE mu = 200 * 0.1.\nCOMPUTE sigma = SQRT(200 * 0.1 * 0.9).\nCOMPUTE with_cc = CDF.NORMAL(14.5, mu, sigma).\nCOMPUTE without_cc = CDF.NORMAL(14, mu, sigma).\nFORMATS exact with_cc without_cc (F8.4).\nLIST exact with_cc without_cc.","note":"CDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is the exact answer; CDF.NORMAL(value, mean, sd) is the approximation."},"stata":{"code":"display binomial(200, 14, 0.1)\ndisplay normal((14.5 - 20) / sqrt(200 * 0.1 * 0.9))\ndisplay normal((14 - 20) / sqrt(200 * 0.1 * 0.9))","note":"binomial(n,k,p) is the exact P(X <= k). The two normal() lines standardise the cut-off by hand, with and without the half-unit correction."},"try":["Change p to 0.02 and n to 200. np is now 4: predict whether the normal approximation gets better or worse, then run it.","Try n = 20 with p = 0.5 and compare the exact and approximate answers for P(X <= 8)."]});
